## Why forms are different in React

Forms look simple in HTML, but real applications usually need to do more than collect values. You may need to validate an email address, enable or disable a button, display an error, submit data to an API, or reset the form after submission.

React gives you a useful way to keep the form values in state and let the UI reflect that state.

For example, an input can be connected directly to state:

~~~jsx
function NameField() {
  const [name, setName] = React.useState("");

  return (
    <input
      value={name}
      onChange={event => setName(event.target.value)}
    />
  );
}
~~~

As the user types, `onChange` updates the state. React then renders the input with the new value.

This is called a **controlled input**.

---

## Controlled inputs

A controlled input is an input whose current value is controlled by React state.

~~~diagram
{"type": "flow", "title": "A controlled input is a loop", "caption": "React state is the single source of truth: the input only ever shows what the state says.", "nodes": [{"id": "u", "label": "User types", "kind": "user"}, {"id": "i", "label": "<input>", "sub": "value={email}", "kind": "client"}, {"id": "h", "label": "onChange", "sub": "setEmail(e.target.value)", "kind": "code"}, {"id": "s", "label": "state: email", "kind": "code", "highlight": true}], "edges": [{"from": "u", "to": "i", "label": "keystroke"}, {"from": "i", "to": "h", "label": "event"}, {"from": "h", "to": "s", "label": "update"}, {"from": "s", "to": "i", "label": "re-render: value={email}", "dashed": true}]}
~~~

The basic pattern is:

~~~jsx
const [email, setEmail] = React.useState("");

return (
  <input
    value={email}
    onChange={event => setEmail(event.target.value)}
  />
);
~~~

There are two important parts:

- `value` tells React what the input should display.
- `onChange` updates the state when the user changes the input.

This gives your component a reliable representation of what the user has entered.

You can then use the same state for validation or other UI decisions:

~~~jsx
const isValid = email.includes("@");

<button disabled={!isValid}>Continue</button>
~~~

This is one of the main advantages of controlled inputs: the form's data is already available to the component.

---

## Uncontrolled inputs

An **uncontrolled input** lets the DOM keep track of the current value rather than storing every change in React state.

For example:

~~~jsx
<input defaultValue="Hello" />
~~~

You can access an uncontrolled input through a ref when you need its value:

~~~jsx
const inputRef = React.useRef(null);

return <input ref={inputRef} />;
~~~

Later, you could read:

~~~js
inputRef.current.value
~~~

Uncontrolled inputs can be useful for some forms and integrations, but controlled inputs are a very common choice when the UI needs to react immediately to what the user types.

For the exercises and most beginner React forms, controlled inputs are the easiest pattern to understand.

---

## Keeping several fields in one state object

A form often contains several related values. Instead of creating separate state variables for every field, you can keep them in one object.

For example:

~~~jsx
const [form, setForm] = React.useState({
  name: "",
  email: "",
  country: ""
});
~~~

When the name changes, preserve the other fields with the spread operator:

~~~jsx
function handleNameChange(event) {
  setForm(previousForm => ({
    ...previousForm,
    name: event.target.value
  }));
}
~~~

The same pattern works for email:

~~~jsx
function handleEmailChange(event) {
  setForm(previousForm => ({
    ...previousForm,
    email: event.target.value
  }));
}
~~~

You could also make one reusable handler if your input names match the state properties:

~~~jsx
function handleChange(event) {
  const { name, value } = event.target;

  setForm(previousForm => ({
    ...previousForm,
    [name]: value
  }));
}
~~~

Then:

~~~jsx
<input name="name" value={form.name} onChange={handleChange} />
<input name="email" value={form.email} onChange={handleChange} />
~~~

The square brackets in `[name]` create a computed property name. If `name` is `email`, the update changes `form.email`.

---

## Checkboxes

Checkboxes work slightly differently from text inputs. Their important property is `checked`, not `value`.

A controlled checkbox looks like this:

~~~jsx
const [accepted, setAccepted] = React.useState(false);

return (
  <input
    type="checkbox"
    checked={accepted}
    onChange={event => setAccepted(event.target.checked)}
  />
);
~~~

Use `event.target.checked` to get the boolean state of the checkbox.

For example, a terms checkbox could be:

~~~jsx
<label>
  <input
    type="checkbox"
    checked={accepted}
    onChange={event => setAccepted(event.target.checked)}
  />
  I accept the terms
</label>
~~~

Now the component always knows whether the user has accepted the terms.

---

## Select elements

A `<select>` can also be controlled with state.

~~~jsx
const [country, setCountry] = React.useState("");

return (
  <select
    value={country}
    onChange={event => setCountry(event.target.value)}
  >
    <option value="">Choose a country</option>
    <option value="hu">Hungary</option>
    <option value="sk">Slovakia</option>
    <option value="de">Germany</option>
  </select>
);
~~~

The same pattern appears again: React owns the current value, and `onChange` updates it.

This makes selects behave consistently with other controlled fields.

---

## Handling form submission

A form should normally use the form's `onSubmit` event rather than putting all submission logic on the button.

~~~jsx
function SignupForm() {
  function handleSubmit(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    console.log("Submit form");
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input />
      <button type="submit">Sign up</button>
    </form>
  );
}
~~~

`preventDefault()` stops the browser's default form submission behaviour, which normally navigates or reloads the page.

React can then handle the submission itself:

~~~text
user clicks Submit
       ↓
form onSubmit runs
       ↓
preventDefault()
       ↓
validate data
       ↓
submit to API
       ↓
show result
~~~

This is particularly important for single-page applications where you usually do not want the browser to reload the entire page.

---

## Validation

Validation checks whether the values entered by the user meet your requirements.

Imagine a sign-up form requiring:

- A name with at least two non-space characters.
- An email address with a valid-looking format.
- Acceptance of the terms.

You can calculate these conditions directly from state:

~~~jsx
const validName = form.name.trim().length >= 2;
const validEmail = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(form.email);
const validTerms = form.terms;

const formIsValid = validName && validEmail && validTerms;
~~~

The regular expression here is deliberately simple. It checks for something before `@`, something between `@` and `.`, and something after the dot.

Email validation can become much more complicated in the real world. For a normal client-side form, you usually want a practical validation rule rather than trying to perfectly reproduce every possible valid email address.

You can use the result to disable the submit button:

~~~jsx
<button type="submit" disabled={!formIsValid}>
  Sign up
</button>
~~~

This gives the user immediate feedback that the form is not ready yet.

---

## Showing errors after the field was touched

There is an important UX problem with showing every error immediately.

~~~diagram
{"type": "steps", "title": "When to show an error", "steps": [{"label": "Untouched", "sub": "no errors yet"}, {"label": "Typing", "sub": "stay quiet"}, {"label": "Blur", "sub": "field is touched → validate"}, {"label": "Submit", "sub": "mark all touched, focus first error"}]}
~~~

Imagine the user opens a registration form and sees:

~~~text
Enter a valid email address
~~~

before they have even touched the email field. That is usually not very helpful.

A common pattern is to track whether a field has been **touched**. This means the user has interacted with the field, usually by focusing it and then leaving it.

For example:

~~~jsx
const [touched, setTouched] = React.useState(false);
~~~

You can mark the field as touched when it loses focus:

~~~jsx
<input
  value={email}
  onChange={event => setEmail(event.target.value)}
  onBlur={() => setTouched(true)}
/>
~~~

Then only display the error when there is a non-empty invalid value and the field has been touched:

~~~jsx
const emailInvalid = touched && email !== "" && !validEmail;
~~~

The UI can then show the error conditionally.

This pattern avoids overwhelming users while still giving them useful feedback after they interact with the field.

---

## Accessible validation errors

Validation messages should not only be visible to people who can see the screen. They should also be connected to the relevant input for assistive technology.

Suppose you have:

~~~jsx
<input
  id="email"
  aria-invalid={emailInvalid}
  aria-describedby={emailInvalid ? "email-error" : undefined}
/>

{emailInvalid && (
  <p id="email-error" role="alert">
    Enter a valid email address
  </p>
)}
~~~

`aria-invalid` tells assistive technology that the current value is invalid.

`aria-describedby` associates the input with additional descriptive content, in this case the error message.

The `role="alert"` can make an important dynamically displayed message available to assistive technologies.

Good form UX is not only about making the form look correct. The form should also communicate its state clearly to people using different input methods and assistive technologies.

---

## Resetting a form

After a successful submission, you may want to clear the form.

If the form is controlled, resetting it is straightforward because React owns the values:

~~~jsx
setForm({
  name: "",
  email: "",
  terms: false
});
~~~

You may also need to reset validation state:

~~~jsx
setTouched({
  email: false
});
~~~

The important idea is that the UI should return to the same state as a newly opened form.

For larger forms, it can be useful to define the initial state once:

~~~jsx
const initialForm = {
  name: "",
  email: "",
  terms: false
};

const [form, setForm] = React.useState(initialForm);

function resetForm() {
  setForm(initialForm);
}
~~~

---

## Submit only valid data

Disabling a button is useful, but it should not be your only validation layer.

For example:

~~~jsx
function handleSubmit(event) {
  event.preventDefault();

  if (!formIsValid) {
    return;
  }

  // Submit the data
}
~~~

This protects the submission handler if it is triggered in another way or if the form logic changes.

You can then prepare the data for your API:

~~~jsx
const payload = {
  name: form.name.trim(),
  email: form.email.trim(),
  termsAccepted: form.terms
};
~~~

Notice that the name and email are trimmed before submission. The data sent to the backend does not necessarily need to be identical to the raw text the user entered.

---

## A complete small form

Here is a simplified example combining the main ideas:

~~~jsx
function SignupForm() {
  const [form, setForm] = React.useState({
    name: "",
    email: "",
    terms: false
  });
  const [emailTouched, setEmailTouched] = React.useState(false);
  const [submitted, setSubmitted] = React.useState(false);

  const validName = form.name.trim().length >= 2;
  const validEmail = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(form.email);
  const emailInvalid = emailTouched && form.email !== "" && !validEmail;
  const formIsValid = validName && validEmail && form.terms;

  function handleChange(event) {
    const { name, value, type, checked } = event.target;

    setForm(previousForm => ({
      ...previousForm,
      [name]: type === "checkbox" ? checked : value
    }));
  }

  function handleSubmit(event) {
    event.preventDefault();

    if (!formIsValid) {
      return;
    }

    setSubmitted(true);
  }

  if (submitted) {
    return <p>Thanks, {form.name.trim()}!</p>;
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <label htmlFor="name">Name</label>
      <input
        id="name"
        name="name"
        value={form.name}
        onChange={handleChange}
      />

      <label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
      <input
        id="email"
        name="email"
        type="email"
        value={form.email}
        onChange={handleChange}
        onBlur={() => setEmailTouched(true)}
        aria-invalid={emailInvalid}
        aria-describedby={emailInvalid ? "email-error" : undefined}
      />

      {emailInvalid && (
        <p id="email-error" role="alert">
          Enter a valid email address
        </p>
      )}

      <label>
        <input
          id="terms"
          name="terms"
          type="checkbox"
          checked={form.terms}
          onChange={handleChange}
        />
        I accept the terms
      </label>

      <button type="submit" disabled={!formIsValid}>
        Sign up
      </button>
    </form>
  );
}
~~~

This is already enough to build many small forms. In a larger application, you will probably also need submission loading state, server-side validation errors, and API integration.

---

## React Hook Form

For larger or more complicated forms, libraries such as **React Hook Form** can reduce repetitive form state and validation code. You do not need a form library to understand React forms, though; learning controlled inputs first gives you the foundation for understanding what these libraries are doing for you.

---

## The backend must validate too

Client-side validation improves the user experience, but it is **not a security boundary**. A user can bypass browser validation or send a request directly to your API.

Your backend must validate the data again before accepting it. Later, when you build Spring Boot backends in module 5, you will see how server-side validation protects the actual API and application data.

Think of it as two layers:

~~~text
React validation
    ↓
Better user experience
    ↓
Backend validation
    ↓
Actual application protection
~~~

Never assume that because React says a value is valid, the backend can safely trust it.

---

## Common mistakes

### Forgetting to control the input correctly

If you provide `value` without an `onChange` handler, the user may not be able to edit the input normally. Controlled inputs need both sides of the connection.

### Using value for a checkbox

For controlled checkboxes, use `checked` and read `event.target.checked`. Text inputs use `value` and `event.target.value`.

### Forgetting preventDefault

Without `event.preventDefault()`, the browser performs its default form submission behaviour, which can reload or navigate away from your React application.

### Showing errors too early

Showing every validation message before the user interacts with a field can make a form feel hostile. Tracking touched fields gives you more control over when errors appear.

### Relying only on a disabled button

Client-side UI restrictions can be bypassed. Validate again in the submit handler and always validate again on the backend.

### Forgetting accessibility

An error that is visible on screen is not automatically connected to the input for assistive technology. Use labels, `aria-invalid`, `aria-describedby`, and appropriate error messaging.

### Mutating a form state object

Do not directly change `form.email` or another property. Create a new object with the spread operator and update it through the state setter.

---

## The bigger picture

React forms are mostly about keeping three things in sync: the user's input, your validation rules, and the UI that communicates the current state.

Controlled inputs give React access to the current values. Event handlers update those values. Validation determines whether the data is acceptable, while touched state can control when errors become visible. On submission, `preventDefault()` keeps the application in control of the process.

As your applications grow, libraries can reduce repetitive form code, and your Spring Boot backend will provide the second validation layer that the application ultimately has to trust.

